Wine cross-process child window surfaces and Steam CEF routing.

LGPL-2.1-or-later. Adapted from typester/sake patches 0005 and 0006,
including the child-window implementation attributed there to James in Wine
bug 60263, attachment 82030. Original copyright and licensing headers remain
in the upstream files. Those patches explicitly disclose AI assistance.

Origins:
https://github.com/typester/sake/blob/main/patches/0005-winemac-cross-process-child-window-swapchains.patch
https://github.com/typester/sake/blob/main/patches/0006-winemac-give-D3DMetal-a-hosted-swapchain-for-a-window-it-does-not-own.patch

Scotch adaptation: WineForge 0.6.0.3 / Wine 11.16's client surface API and
window destruction; route only Steam's cef.win64 helper through the matched
DXMT runtime, retaining the game's configured backend. Extend WineForge's
existing renderer copy-on-write compatibility to that helper's renderer only.
No Valve files, checksums or TLS settings are modified.

Original upstream provenance and licensing statements (unaltered):

winemac: host cross-process child window Metal swapchains at the child's rect in the root's window.

The reference implementation attached to Wine bug 60263 (attachment 82030, by "James",
2026-09-03), which its author regenerated against a pristine wine 11.16 tree and offers
under the licence of the files it touches. It takes what 0004 does for a top-level window
to the child windows Chromium actually draws into: a child of another process has no
NSWindow of its own, so its CAContext is posted to the root that has one, positioned at
the child's rectangle in the root's space, stacked in Win32 paint order, and moved when
the child moves. Steam's GPU process asks for exactly that -- Chrome_WidgetWin_1 inside
CefBrowserWindow inside the browser's SDL_app top-level -- and without it the FIXME that
0004 leaves for child windows is what every Steam start hits (docs/runtime.md,
2026-09-20).

Rebased onto the 26.3.0 tree with 0003 and 0004 in: one hunk placed by hand, the release
of remote_layer_children in macdrv_DestroyWindow, because CrossOver's function has a
capture-release line where upstream's context expects drag_event. The author's own
header follows unchanged; its licensing statement is the one this file is under, and it
marks itself as written with AI assistance. sake carries it as attributed rather than
rewriting it.

